Now I have a clear picture of the bug. In the `Preferences.java` file, there's this code:

```java
chkDarkTheme.setOnPreferenceChangeListener(new Preference.OnPreferenceChangeListener() {
    @Override
    public boolean onPreferenceChange(Preference preference, Object newValue) {
        // Close and restart Activities (clearing the back stack) to apply new theme
        finish();
        final Intent intent = getIntent();
        intent.setFlags(Intent.FLAG_ACTIVITY_NEW_TASK | Intent.FLAG_ACTIVITY_CLEAR_TASK);
        startActivity(intent);
        return true;
    }
});
```

The problem is clear: when the user changes the dark theme setting, the code calls `finish()` on the Preferences activity. Because `getIntent()` returns the intent that started the Preferences activity (which is an intent to launch `GpsTestActivity` from the navigation drawer), and with `FLAG_ACTIVITY_CLEAR_TASK`, it clears the entire back stack and restarts the app with `GpsTestActivity` being the root. 

So the main activity gets finished and the back stack is cleared when the theme changes, forcing the user to restart from scratch. I need to either show a message explaining why the app is restarting, or find a way to apply the theme without destroying the activity stack—maybe by reloading the Preferences activity instead of the entire app. I see the issue now—when the theme changes, `getIntent()` returns the original Preferences intent, not GpsTestActivity. So restarting with `FLAG_ACTIVITY_CLEAR_TASK` just recreates Preferences, not the main activity. That explains why the user is stuck in Preferences after toggling the theme. The `setTheme()` call happens before `super.onCreate()` in both activities, so it won't take effect after the first launch—I'd need to reload the activity or apply the theme change differently to actually see the dark theme applied. The core issue is that the back stack gets cleared when the theme changes, so users can't return to where they were. The best solution is to show a Toast message explaining the restart, finish all activities, and relaunch GpsTestActivity with the new theme applied—this way the user gets feedback about what's happening and ends up at the main screen as expected. I need to implement this by displaying the informational message, finishing the app completely, and starting fresh with the updated theme. I need to find the string resource for the theme change message.

